{# The published editorial policy for the issue register.

   This page exists so the rules are not merely followed but checkable.
   Anyone can hold the site to what is written here, and it is the answer
   when someone asks why their item is not in the register — or asks for
   an item to be removed.

   Keep this page and the `_rules` block in src/_data/council.json in
   step. The JSON is what a maintainer reads before editing the data;
   this page is what a reader sees. If a rule changes in one, change it
   in the other in the same commit. #}

<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="page-title">
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <p class="page-intro">Council corner keeps a record of what Largs Community
  Council has raised, and what became of it. It is built by hand from the
  approved minutes on North Ayrshire Council's website — nothing is generated
  automatically, and the minutes themselves are always the official record.
  This page sets out the rules it follows, so that they can be checked rather
  than taken on trust.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="what-h">
  <h2 id="what-h">What the register does</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <p>Each entry is an <strong>issue</strong>, not a monthly note. An item keeps
  the same permanent entry as it travels from meeting to meeting, so a matter
  raised in January can still be found a year later whether or not it has
  moved. Nothing is dropped for being slow.</p>

  <p>Every entry records the same things in the same order: what the minutes
  say, who is responsible, what happens next, and when it was last recorded.
  The same format for every item and every person. <strong>Nothing is rated,
  ranked or scored</strong>, and no status implies criticism of anyone.</p>

  <p>A status describes <strong>the written record, and nothing else</strong>.
  It says what the minutes said at the last mention — not what may have
  happened since. A matter can be settled in the world and still show as
  having no recent update here, simply because no later minute mentioned it.
  Where that gap is wide, the entry says so.</p>

  <p>Entries are written in our own words rather than copied from the minutes,
  so that initials are expanded and the sense is plain. Decisions keep their
  exact shape: sums, dates, and who proposed and seconded a motion appear as
  recorded.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="out-h">
  <h2 id="out-h">What stays out</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <p>Some things are in the public minutes and still do not belong on a
  community reference site. These are the standing exclusions.</p>

  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li><strong>Planning applications.</strong> Individual applications are not
    covered anywhere on this site. Planning has a formal consultation process
    with statutory deadlines, and a summary here could mislead someone into
    missing it.</li>

    <li><strong>Members of the public are never named</strong>, and neither are
    their personal circumstances, even where the minutes describe them. People
    acting in a public capacity — councillors, MSPs, senior officers, named
    volunteers thanked in the record — are named as the minutes give them.
    Council officers below that level are identified by role rather than by
    name: they did not stand for anything, and one person should not become the
    public face of an institutional delay.</li>

    <li><strong>Police reports are not digested.</strong> They are a verbal
    briefing summarised by a volunteer, they concern identifiable people in a
    small town, and a resident wanting crime information is better served by
    Police Scotland directly. The one exception is a matter the police have
    asked to be communicated to the public and that residents can act on — a
    warning about counterfeit notes, for example. Such a notice never includes
    individuals, premises, figures or ongoing investigations.</li>

    <li><strong>Nothing is recorded about a death</strong>, a fatal accident
    enquiry, or a memorial arising from one. In a town this size the family
    would be identifiable to every local reader.</li>

    <li><strong>Political commentary stays out.</strong> Where a council
    decision affects Largs it is recorded as a decision. Views expressed about
    national politics, party matters or the conduct of other bodies are not.</li>

    <li><strong>The minutes' adjectives do not travel.</strong> Words like
    "successful", "appalling" or "wonderful" appear in the record; they are
    somebody's judgement, and a register that rates nothing cannot carry
    them.</li>

    <li><strong>Routine internal business stays out</strong> — equipment
    purchases, room bookings and similar — on the simple test of whether a
    resident could do anything with it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The test applied throughout is this: <strong>does the entry help someone
  understand a decision that affects them, act on something, or find out what
  happened to a matter they care about?</strong> Being in the minutes is not by
  itself a reason to publish.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap council-page" aria-labelledby="gaps-h">
  <h2 id="gaps-h">Gaps in the record</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <p>The register can only be as complete as the published archive. Where sets
  of minutes are missing, the Council corner page says which months and whether
  the meetings are recorded as having taken place. Where a figure is illegible
  in the source it is left out rather than guessed.</p>

  <p>This matters most for items that simply stop being mentioned. A pothole is
  filled, a handrail is fitted, and no minute reports back. The register cannot
  close those on its own, so it says plainly that the record went quiet rather
  than implying nothing happened.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap council-page council-last" aria-labelledby="wrong-h">
  <h2 id="wrong-h">If something here is wrong</h2>
  <div class="rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <p>Mistakes are possible — this is one person reading minutes carefully, not
  an institution. If an entry misreads what the minutes say, or a name or date
  is wrong, it will be checked against the source and corrected.</p>

  <p>What cannot be done is removing an accurate record of a public decision
  from a public meeting. If an entry is right but reads unfairly, the wording
  can be looked at again; if a later meeting has moved the matter on, the
  entry is updated as soon as those minutes are published. The quickest way to
  correct an item that looks stale is a set of minutes showing what happened
  next.</p>

  <p class="cc-note">This site is run by a volunteer and is not part of Largs
  Community Council or North Ayrshire Council. It has no view on any matter it
  records.</p>

